{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And I will come down to deliver it… to a good and broad land.</b> Why does Scripture need to elaborate so at length in praise of its trees and stones? And further, why recount the praise in this place at all — for a people whose soul has become iron, afflicted with suffering under harsh masters? Is joy and gladness not enough for them, to be given tidings of redemption, even on condition of going forth to a land of troubles? But the matter is that all these praises are said on account of the Torah: 'a good and broad land' — a land of goodness and of breadth. 'Good,' as it is said (Proverbs 4:2): ״כי לקח טוב״ — 'for a good teaching'; 'broad,' as it is said (Psalms 119:96): ״רחבה מצותך מאד״ — 'Your commandment is exceedingly broad,' and it is written (Job 11:9): ״ורחבה מני ים״ — 'broader than the sea'; 'a land flowing with milk and honey,' as it is said (Song of Songs 4:11): ״דבש וחלב״ — 'honey and milk.' And afterward it mentions the land itself, for there is the root of this Torah — this is what it means by 'to the place of the Canaanite,' that is, you shall inherit the place of the Canaanites, for they are not worthy to dwell in the place of the Torah's dwelling. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Genesis 2:11–12): ״וזהב הארץ ההיא טוב״ — 'and the gold of that land is good' — there is no Torah like the Torah of the Land of Israel, and no wisdom like the wisdom of the Land of Israel. And one may further expound 'to the place of the Canaanite' — it did not say 'to the land of the Canaanite,' as it says in other places, to hint that they shall inherit them and cut them off and dwell in their place, not that they shall dwell among them as their fathers did."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:48.375901343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}